# Claude seat — round 2 (Architecture & systems)

I verified the three contested code facts this round rather than argue them: codex's Q2 seat-selection claim, the §11 "completeness check" trust surface behind Q1, and the "first numeric invariant" claim behind Q5. All three land. My round-1 **caution** holds, but it is now sharper and one sub-ruling has moved toward codex.

## 1. Updated verdict

**CAUTION — proceed, but fold Q1 (mechanized merge reconciliation), Q2 (unique-id chair), and Q5 (two-artifact split) into D15+ before any Phase 2 code.** Confidence: **high**. What would change it: mechanize the chair's completeness check as an INV-1-style reconciliation (Q1) and split `rubric.json` from `scorecard.json` (Q5), and I move to ship — those are the only two design-level defects; everything else is refinement.

## 2. Per-question rulings (Q1–Q9)

**Q1 — CAUTION.** The single proposal→merge shape and the ≥2-usable floor are right and well-precedented. But "the conductor cross-asserts completeness (every proposal accounted for)" is **not mechanizable as worded** — there is no chair-emitted mapping to check, so the conductor is *trusting* the chair to merge honestly, the exact thing §11 forbids ("completeness checks … conductor-computed and never model-trusted"). The fix is not new invention: `reconcile_edits` (INV-1) already does this for revision. Require the chair to emit an explicit mapping — each merged criterion → the proposal-id(s) it subsumes; each dropped proposal-id → reason — and have the conductor verify the **partition mechanically**: every proposal-id appears exactly once across (subsumed ∪ dropped) [coverage / no omission] and every merged criterion cites only real proposal-ids [no phantom / no invention]. Trade-off: one cheap conductor check versus a §11 violation at the feature's central new trust surface.

**Q2 — CAUTION.** Strawman (a)'s wording — "reuse the synthesizer-selection rules verbatim … as `choose_revision_seat` already did" — conflates two *different* selection axes and picks the wrong one. `choose_synthesizer_seat` keys on **provider name** (`by_name = {s.name: s …}`, synthesizer.py:220; `s.name` is the provider, resolve_board:371), so on a legitimate duplicate-provider board (`claude` + `claude#2`) the dict silently collapses to the last same-named seat and `--synthesizer-seat claude` cannot disambiguate — there is no `resolve_synthesizer_seat_id` front door. `choose_revision_seat` uses the **unique-id axis** (`by_id` first, revision.py:280–283) behind `resolve_revision_seat_id`, which *refuses* an ambiguous provider name (config.py:432). Mirror the **revision** path (a `resolve_chair_seat_id` + id-first `choose_chair_seat`), not the synthesizer's. Scoring-as-acceptance is correct (no extra endorse fan-out); add codex's criterion-scoped `RUBRIC-NOTE cN:`. Trade-off: a few lines of id-resolver reuse now versus a latent wrong-chair selection on aliased boards.

**Q3 — SHIP.** Coexistence (b) is the only viable path; I converge with gemini here. Scores are additive structure that leaves the token chain (gate, convergence, synthesizer, schema) untouched, versus a score-only fork that would double every downstream mode and make rubric runs incomparable with all prior runs. 1–5 integers (coarse, moots the epsilon question), conductor-assigned `c1…cN` at merge, `SCORE cN:` lines each hardened by `parse_verdict`'s per-line discipline. All correct.

**Q4 — SHIP, with one addition.** Informational-only at the gate for v1.15 is right — a gameable number must not move a gate (the `confidence` precedent), and no calibration data exists to tune bands against. But surface the token↔band contradiction in the **primary verdict summary**, not only deep in the scorecard, or the "auditable against stated standards" promise reads hollow exactly when it matters. Record (b) — the ABSTAIN-consistency check — as the explicit next milestone once real scored runs calibrate the bands. The "no teeth" worry is overstated: scores *do* bite through convergence (Q6), just not through the gate. Trade-off: honest-but-non-gating now versus an un-calibrated threshold formula (false precision, §11).

**Q5 — CAUTION.** Separate artifact of record: yes. But **split it in two** — `rubric.json` (criteria, weights, `proposed_by[]`, drops-with-reason, merge provenance) written at **chair-merge time** (post-consent per RH-1, *before* the opinion rounds), and `scorecard.json` (per-round scores, notes, conductor-computed totals/bands) written after. The split is forced by the pipeline's temporal shape, not aesthetics: artifacts are write-once and sha-pinned, yet the rubric must be persisted *before* the rounds it is injected into and must **survive a later scoring failure** (Q9's protect-produced-value logic) — a single write-once file cannot hold both merge-time criteria and post-round scores. Each mirrors `board_changes.py` discipline (unknown keys refused, model- vs tool-authored fields enumerated, strict-when-present pointer). State the **weight-sum-to-100** rule loudly: it is the **first numeric-sum invariant** in the codebase — conductor-computed, integer, reject-on-violation, test-guarded. Per-round scores (the trajectory is the convergence story): yes. Trade-off: two validators to maintain versus an artifact that cannot persist the rubric before the rounds depend on it.

**Q6 — SHIP.** Widen the one movement boolean: `verdict_shift OR new_cites OR score_changed` (convergence.py:197–209). Integer scores make any change ≥1, so the epsilon problem never arises; oscillation is bounded by `--max-rounds` exactly as token-flapping already is. One edge to specify: a criterion **absent in both rounds** is *not* movement (else a chronically-missing score blocks convergence forever); appearance, disappearance, or change of a score *is* movement. Surface which criteria are still moving in the round-done detail (`movement_detail_line`, convergence.py:238 already builds that string). Trade-off: one unified definition versus a bespoke score-convergence stop rule with new failure modes and the "agree on numbers, still flip tokens" gap.

**Q7 — SHIP.** Orthogonal boolean, recipe-recorded (it changes record-artifact shape — the `synthesize` precedent), own STAGES token, RH-1-respecting, `--from-recipe`-replayable, no hard `--synthesize` requirement. On the unpinned-scorecard tampering worry: it's consistent with existing posture — round artifacts aren't sha-pinned by the verdict either, and the strawman's own analogy ("stands alone like round artifacts do") holds. Refinement, not a blocker: if a run manifest exists, record the `rubric.json`/`scorecard.json` shas there so there's a tool-authored integrity anchor even when synthesis doesn't run. Trade-off: an unpinned-but-anchored standalone artifact versus forcing `--synthesize` purely to mint a pointer.

**Q8 — SHIP (c).** Ship `stakeholder-panel` (and optionally `investor-pitch`) as `LENS_PRESETS` entries; structured personas are a later milestone. My round-1 cost caveat is **resolved by the code**: per-seat *positional* lensing already exists (`lens = lens_overrides.get(sid) or (lenses[index] if index < len(lenses) else lenses[-1])`, resolve_board:367), and every `LENS_PRESETS` entry is already a 3-string list (constants.py:71–102) — so a three-archetype panel (CFO / end-user / compliance) genuinely ships in an afternoon on existing machinery. One documented caveat: archetypes bind to **seat order** and degrade on non-3 boards (a 4th seat repeats the last voice; a 2-seat board drops the 3rd). Don't let the preset silently imply `--rubric`; document the `--rubric --lens stakeholder-panel` combination. Trade-off: prose personas on existing rails versus a third preset axis that would pre-empt the seats' own proposal pass.

**Q9 — CAUTION (mostly ship, one refinement).** Proposal-floor refusal before any opinion round: ship — preflight-style NO-GO, nothing produced to protect. **Chair-merge failure → refuse (ii): correct, and *not* actually at odds with the codebase.** The reflex is "failure never discards **successful** work"; at chair-merge time no opinion round has run, so there is no successful work — the synthesizer degrades gracefully precisely because it fails *after* the expensive rounds. Silently degrading to a plain run delivers a different deliberation shape than the user asked for (the R4 "one voice wearing the board's name" objection). Refinement: exhaust the seat-selection fallback chain (next usable seat) before writing `rubric-rejected.json`, so one flaky CLI doesn't sink the run. Scoring lines — reconcile the retry tension codex flagged: **usability is defined by the VERDICT token, unchanged**; a missing/invalid SCORE triggers the standard completeness retry but then degrades to a *partial scorecard* (`—`, never imputed), never to an unusable seat — so the gate/convergence axis stays exactly as robust as today. Q9b carry-forward: ship (mechanical digest, §11 no-re-reasoning). Trade-off: refuse-on-chair-failure looks like it breaks the never-fail reflex, but the reflex protects produced value, of which there is none yet.

## 3. Where I changed my mind, and where I still dissent

- **Changed, toward codex (Q2).** I now require the chair to be chosen on the **unique-id axis** (mirror `choose_revision_seat`/`resolve_revision_seat_id`), explicitly *not* "the synthesizer-selection rules verbatim." Exact reason codex surfaced and I verified: `choose_synthesizer_seat` keys on provider (`by_name`, synthesizer.py:220/227) and silently collapses duplicate-provider boards; `choose_revision_seat` keys on id (revision.py:280) behind an ambiguity-refusing resolver (config.py:432). The strawman's wording imports a latent wrong-seat bug.
- **Changed, by my own verification (Q8).** I withdraw my round-1 caveat that a three-voice panel might need new per-seat-lens machinery. It doesn't — resolve_board:367 already lenses positionally per seat. This resolves my own stale fact; it is not deference.
- **Still dissent from gemini (SHIP).** Gemini's "backward-compatible → ship" reasoning correctly covers the token chain but is silent on the two *new* trust surfaces the feature introduces: the chair merge (Q1) and the weight arithmetic + artifact lifetime (Q5). A correct Q3 does not rescue an unmechanized chair or a single-file scorecard that cannot persist the rubric before the rounds depend on it. That is why I hold caution.
- **Hold relative to codex ("would move to ship").** I've adopted all three of codex's conditions, yet stay caution because my Q1 bar is stricter than codex's: codex asks the *chair* not to introduce untraceable criteria (a prompt guardrail, the no-phantom half); I require the *conductor* to verify the full partition mechanically (coverage **and** no-phantom — INV-1 in both directions). That is a design change, not a prompt tweak.

## 4. Strongest remaining objections (in priority order)

1. **Q1 — mechanize the merge reconciliation.** Highest priority. Without a chair-emitted mapping the conductor can check, §11 is violated at the feature's core. INV-1 already proves the pattern; copy it.
2. **Q5 — the two-artifact split is forced, not optional.** `rubric.json` must be written at merge (pre-rounds, post-consent) and survive a later scoring failure; a write-once `scorecard.json` cannot do both.
3. **Q2 — id-axis chair selection**, else duplicate-provider boards silently seat the wrong chair.
4. **Q4 — the token↔band contradiction must appear in the primary summary**, not buried, or the auditability promise is undercut exactly when scores and token disagree.

## 5. Recommended execution sequence (rewrite of the M5 phases)

- **Phase 2a — schema & validator core first** (I agree with gemini's sequencing): `advisory-board/rubric@1` + `board_rubric.py` (the weight-sum-to-100 invariant, unknown-keys-refused, model vs tool fields) and `advisory-board/scorecard@1` + `board_scorecard.py`; both pinned strict-when-present from `verdict.json`; both whole-drop to zero body bytes on non-rubric runs (D5). Land the numeric invariant test-guarded here.
- **Phase 2b — proposal pass:** conductor mints proposal-ids *before* the chair sees proposals; per-seat weight validation; ≥2-usable floor with preflight-style refusal.
- **Phase 2c — chair merge + INV-1 reconciliation together** (build the regression guard *with* the merge, not after): chair on the unique-id axis; chair emits the mapping; conductor verifies the partition + weight arithmetic; on failure exhaust the seat fallback, then `rubric-rejected.json` + exit non-zero.
- **Phase 3 — scoring + convergence:** `SCORE cN` parser (per-line `parse_verdict` hardening); widen the movement boolean; partial-scorecard degrade; VERDICT-defines-usability rule.
- **Phase 4 — gate (informational + primary-summary contradiction row), render (lens-aware, D5 whole-drop), `stakeholder-panel` lens preset, `--revise` carry-forward.**
- Cross-cutting (recipe record + STAGES token + RH-1 + from-recipe replay) rides in with 2b/2c.

## 6. Invariants and guardrails

- **INV-R1 (merge partition, mirrors INV-1):** every proposal-id appears exactly once across (subsumed ∪ dropped); every merged criterion cites ≥1 real proposal-id; no criterion cites a non-existent id; empty-subsumption criteria rejected. Conductor checks structure, never semantics.
- **All ids conductor-minted:** proposal-ids at parse, `c1…cN` at merge. Models never mint identity.
- **INV-R2 (weight invariant):** integer weights sum to exactly 100, conductor-validated, reject-on-violation — the first numeric-sum invariant; test-guarded; no per-criterion precision beyond the weight (echo-score philosophy).
- **Chair on the unique-id axis** (`resolve_chair_seat_id`), duplicate-provider-safe.
- **Usability axis unchanged:** a reply is usable iff it carries a valid VERDICT token; scores are additive/best-effort; missing → `—`, never imputed; the gate reads only tokens.
- **D5 byte-identity:** every rubric render section whole-drops to zero bytes on a non-rubric run; the no-flags default stays byte-identical.
- **RH-1:** first disk write (`rubric.json`) only after consent.
- **Failure protects produced value:** proposal-floor and chair-merge refuse *before* rounds (nothing to protect); scoring/synth failures *after* rounds never discard them.

## 7. Risks, stale assumptions, missing evidence

- **Stale assumption in the brief (Q2):** strawman (a)'s "as `choose_revision_seat` already did" is misleading — `choose_revision_seat` does *not* use the synthesizer's rules; it uses the id axis. Reword the decision to "mirror the revision selection."
- **Resolved ambiguity (Q8):** per-seat vs uniform lensing is settled — per-seat positional. The product-context line ("a lens is a plain prose string injected as the seat's 'Role emphasis'", singular) reads as board-uniform and should be corrected in the record.
- **No calibration data (Q3/Q4):** the 1–5 scale and any band thirds are un-calibrated; shipping scores informational-only and deferring gate integration is the correct hedge. Do not hard-code band semantics into the gate now.
- **Chair-prompt reliability (gemini's challenge to me):** whether the chair reliably emits a strict mapping with drop-reasons is genuinely unproven — which is *exactly why* the conductor must reconcile mechanically. With INV-R1, a phantom or omission becomes a reject+retry, never a bad rubric shipped; prompt-reliability degrades to a retry concern, not a correctness risk.
- **Latency (gemini's objection):** two sequential pre-round calls do add startup cost. Accept it — `--rubric` is opt-in, standards-first is the whole point, and a tier must never silently skip the pass (Q7). Document it; do not "optimize" by dropping the proposal pass (that reintroduces the R4 one-voice failure).

## 8. Concrete evidence

- **§11 vs Q1:** brief §11 — "everything structural (ids, weights arithmetic, **completeness checks**) is conductor-computed and never model-trusted." Q1 — "The conductor cross-asserts completeness (every proposal accounted for)" with no emitted mapping. Direct contradiction.
- **INV-1 precedent (the fix for Q1):** `reconcile_edits`, revision.py:592–596 — "every changed original line and every insertion boundary in the original→revised diff is EXPLAINED by ≥1 edit locator, and every edit locator explains ≥1 real change. Raises RevisionRejected on any discrepancy"; revision.py:558–559 — "no unexplained change … no phantom locator."
- **Q2:** synthesizer.py:220 `by_name = {s.name: s for s in config.board}`; :227 `if "claude" in by_name: return by_name["claude"]` (provider-keyed, collapses duplicates) vs revision.py:280 `by_id = {s.id: s …}`, :283 `by_id.get(preferred) or by_name.get(preferred)`, docstring :274–279 ("`claude#2` selects that exact seat on a duplicate board"), and config.py:415–436 `resolve_revision_seat_id` (die on ambiguous provider name, :432). Why `by_name` == provider: resolve_board:371 `name=provider`. Duplicate ids are real and supported: resolve_board:342–350 (`assign_seat_ids` + duplicate-id `die`).
- **Q8 lens:** resolve_board:365–367 `lens = lens_overrides.get(sid) or (lenses[index] if index < len(lenses) else lenses[-1])`; `LENS_PRESETS` constants.py:71–102 (each preset a 3-string list).
- **Q5 first numeric invariant:** grep of `scripts/` for `sum(` / `== 100` / `weight` returns only *computed* sums (rounds.py:363 usable count; artifacts.py:442 `tokens_sum`; echo_score.py:98 mean) — zero validated sum-to-N constraint. Validator discipline to mirror: board_changes.py:22 "This validator is strict — unknown top-level keys are refused"; :60–61 "Unknown keys are refused so a fabricated/fuzzed artifact can't smuggle fields past the validator (mirrors board_verdict's strict-when-present"; :38 reject path (`changes-rejected.json`); pointer model board_changes.py:7–8 (`verdict.json.changes = {artifact, sha256}`).
- **Q6:** convergence.py:197–209 `seat_movement` — `"moved": bool(verdict_shift or new_cites)`; :238–248 `movement_detail_line`.

BASIS: independent
VERDICT: caution

## Codex — round 2 review

**1. Updated Verdict**
Caution, confidence high. The design is shippable only after D15+ records four changes: chair selection by canonical seat id, an explicit proposal-to-rubric partition, a standalone `rubric.json`/`scorecard.json` artifact chain, and a clarified score-parse retry path. What would change my verdict to ship: those become required invariants in the roadmap before Phase 2 code.

Q map: Q1 ship with merge-partition guardrails; Q2 caution on “reuse synthesizer verbatim”; Q3 ship with a separate scoring parser; Q4 ship only if score/token contradictions stay non-gating but loud; Q5 caution unless `rubric.json` is the pre-round artifact of record; Q6 ship; Q7 ship; Q8 ship; Q9 caution until retry/partial-score semantics are precise.

**2. Changed / Dissent**
Changed toward Claude on Q1: Claude’s “completeness check is unverifiable as worded” is stronger than my round-1 “no untraced criteria” guardrail. The chair must emit a conductor-checkable partition: every proposal id appears exactly once in either a merged criterion’s `subsumes[]` or a dropped entry with reason.

Still dissent from Gemini’s overall SHIP: the latency concern is real but opt-in; the blocking risk is structural ambiguity, not cost. The current code has duplicate-provider seat ids and strict artifact precedents that the strawmen do not fully incorporate.

**3. Strongest Remaining Objections**
The phrase “conductor cross-asserts completeness” is under-specified. Without proposal ids and chair-emitted mapping, the conductor can validate JSON shape but not completeness.

`--chair-seat` cannot mirror current synthesizer lookup verbatim. `choose_synthesizer_seat()` keys preferred seats by provider name, which collapses duplicate-provider boards; chair/revision must use canonical ids.

Q3 and Q9 conflict operationally. “Missing score triggers standard retry” plus “seat remains usable after retry” needs a purpose-built rubric round runner or classifier. Current retry machinery drops `InvalidOutput` after the second attempt.

`rubric.json` should be a first-class artifact, not just fields embedded in `scorecard.json`. The agreed rubric and proposal accounting are the record that later scoring depends on.

**4. Recommended Execution Sequence**
1. Add design decisions first: chair id semantics, merge partition, artifact chain, score retry/partial semantics, non-gating scores.
2. Build parsers/validators before orchestration: proposal parser, chair merge parser, `board_rubric.py` or rubric validation inside `board_scorecard.py`, then `board_scorecard.py`.
3. Add rubric proposal + chair module before round 1, inside the existing post-activate guard.
4. Add scoring parser and rubric-aware round runner, with tests for partial score cells after retry.
5. Add convergence score movement, recipe/from-recipe, live status stage, render sections, and byte-identity tests.
6. Add stakeholder-panel as a lens preset only.
7. Dogfood rubric runs before release, especially duplicate-provider boards and `--revise`.

**5. Invariants And Guardrails**
All ids are conductor-minted: proposal ids, criterion ids `c1..cN`, canonical chair seat id.

Merge partition: every proposal id appears exactly once across `subsumes[]` and `drops[]`; every merged criterion cites at least one proposal; every drop has a reason.

Weights are positive integer percentages and sum exactly 100 after chair merge. Invalid arithmetic retries the chair, then refuses.

`VERDICT:` remains canonical for gate and schema. Scores never derive or override verdict tokens in v1.15.

Score parser uses `parse_verdict`-style hardening per criterion id: quoted/indented/code-spanned score lines rejected, last qualifying line per id wins, no imputation.

Missing score cells after final retry are absent cells, not zeroes; totals are marked partial.

Non-rubric runs must keep rendered body byte identity. New rubric sections whole-drop to zero bytes.

`--from-recipe` replays rubric/chair templates and canonical chair id exactly. `--revise` mechanically carries the prior rubric.

**6. Risks / Missing Evidence**
No calibration data exists for score bands or token/score contradiction labels. Keep them descriptive, not gating.

The exact exit code for chair-merge refusal is still ambiguous: “preflight NO-GO-like” versus a new rubric integrity failure. Decide before implementation.

The chair prompt may still hallucinate valid-looking mappings. The partition invariant catches structural invention, not semantic bad merges.

Scorecard rendering could become noisy with per-round, per-seat, per-criterion rows. The artifact should keep full data; human render should summarize with drill-down style sections.

Stakeholder-panel as a lens preset satisfies the current machinery, but not the fuller “board composition preset” product vision. That should be documented as out of scope.

**7. Concrete Evidence**
`skills/advisory-board/scripts/_conductor/cli.py:421` defines `_run_after_activate`; round 1 begins immediately at `cli.py:435-437`, so rubric insertion is genuinely new pre-round structure.

`skills/advisory-board/scripts/_conductor/convergence.py:90-112` parses one `VERDICT:` token; `convergence.py:197-210` defines movement only as verdict shift or new citations.

`skills/advisory-board/scripts/_conductor/synthesizer.py:220-228` selects preferred synthesizer by `s.name`; `revision.py:274-288` explains canonical unique-id selection for duplicate boards.

`skills/advisory-board/scripts/_conductor/config.py:300-321` creates unique ids such as `provider#1`; this is why chair selection must not be provider-name only.

`skills/advisory-board/scripts/_conductor/spawn.py:188-222` classifies malformed round output as retryable `InvalidOutput`; `rounds.py:125-135` retries once, then stops. That does not implement “partial scores but usable seat” by itself.

`skills/advisory-board/scripts/board_verdict.py:180-217` validates `changes` as exactly `{artifact, sha256}`; `board_changes.py:294-301` refuses unknown top-level keys. This is the right precedent for scorecard/rubric artifacts.

`skills/advisory-board/scripts/render_handoff.py:45-50` states new sections must whole-drop to zero body bytes when empty.

`skills/advisory-board/scripts/_conductor/status.py:34-37` records RH-1, and `status.py:88` lists the current stage enum without `rubric`.

`skills/advisory-board/scripts/_conductor/recipe.py:240-267` persists record-shaping synth settings and template shas; `recipe.py:282-285` explicitly says live status is not persisted because it is not a record artifact.

BASIS: evidence
VERDICT: caution

## Gemini — round 2 review

### 1. Updated Verdict
- **Verdict**: `CAUTION`
- **Confidence**: `high`
- **What would change it**: I would move to `SHIP` if we explicitly mandate conductor-minted proposal IDs for structural completeness checks, enforce unique seat-ID selection for the chair, and provide a programmatic `--rubric-file <path>` option to bypass pre-round LLM latency.

---

### 2. Changed My Mind & Still Dissent
- **Where I CHANGED MY MIND (aligning with Claude and Codex)**:
  - **With Claude on Q1 (Completeness & Partition Integrity)**: I previously accepted the strawman's unmechanized completeness check. Claude's objection regarding §11 is absolutely correct: without structural constraints, we are blindly trusting the chair's reasoning to not drop ideas or hallucinate criteria. I now agree that the conductor must assign sequential unique proposal IDs (e.g., `p1_seat_id`) during the proposal pass, and require the chair seat's JSON merge output to explicitly partition *every single* proposal ID into a `subsumed` or `dropped` array. The conductor must validate this partition structurally, refusing empty subsumptions or untraced criteria.
  - **With Codex on Q2 (Chair Selection on Unique-ID Axis)**: I previously accepted the synthesizer-style seat lookup. Codex's point that a name-collapsible lookup fails on duplicate-provider boards (e.g., two `claude` seats with different focuses) is a severe regression. The `--chair-seat` selection must resolve against unique seat IDs (e.g., `claude#2`), following `choose_revision_seat`'s robust `by_id` dictionary check.
  - **With Codex on Q1/Q5 (Independent Pre-Round `rubric.json`)**: I now support writing an intermediate `rubric.json` artifact of record immediately after the chair merge step. This decouples rubric definition from execution, allows the rubric state to be audited before the opinion rounds start, and supports resuming from crash/interruption.

- **Where I STILL DISSENT**:
  - **With Claude on Execution Sequence**: Claude proposes writing M5's phases so the parser/proposal validation lands *first* (Phase 2a/2b) and schema validation core (Phase 1) is deferred. I still assert that **Phase 1 must be the Schema & Validator Core** (`board_scorecard.py` and `advisory-board/scorecard@1` validation). Establishing the rigid, validated target contracts for artifacts up-front acts as a regression-guard and prevents "contract drift" during speculative prompt/pipeline engineering.
  - **With the Strawman on Q4 (Gate Integration of Score-Token Contradictions)**: The strawman suggests leaving score-token contradictions purely as a rendered warning (Q4(a)). I dissent: while we must not gate on gameable scores themselves, we must treat severe seat self-contradictions (e.g., top-third weighted score with a `block` token, or bottom-third with `ship`) as a structural anomaly that trips the gate's `ABSTAIN` (exit code 3) path in `--strict` mode. Ignoring this degrades the gate's integrity.
  - **With the Strawman on Q1's Pre-Round Latency**: A two-step sequential LLM pass (Proposal + Chair Merge) before Round 1 begins creates a significant 10–20 second developer-experience bottleneck. To mitigate this, we should support `--rubric-file <path>` as a direct option, allowing developers to supply their own static criteria and completely bypass the pre-round LLM proposal and chair-merge latency.

---

### 3. Strongest Remaining Objections
1. **The §11 "Conductor Plumbs, Models Reason" Boundary Violation in Q1/Q3**: The design must strictly prevent models from inventing numeric parameters or structural definitions. The models propose prose and drop reasons, but the conductor must compute IDs, apply weights arithmetic, and check completeness.
2. **Name-Collapsing Seat Selection in Synthesizer vs. Chair (Q2)**: If we reuse synthesizer seat selection, a multi-model board with duplicate providers will break. We must enforce unique-ID resolution.
3. **The Unmitigated Pre-Round Latency Penalty**: A sequential LLM start-up block of two calls will hurt adoption. We must provide a programmatic `--rubric-file` escape hatch.
4. **Weak Gate integration of scorecard contradictions**: A seat that says "Excellent" (score 5/5) but votes "BLOCK" (or vice versa) is a serious pipeline contradiction. It must escalate to `ABSTAIN` (exit 3) rather than just rendering a silent warning.

---

### 4. Recommended Execution Sequence
1. **Phase 1: Schema & Validator Core**: Author `advisory-board/scorecard@1` and its strict validator `board_scorecard.py` (preventing unknown keys, enforcing that weights sum to exactly 100, and ensuring integer 1–5 scale boundaries).
2. **Phase 2: Proposal Parse & Conductor Partition Checking**: Implement proposal parsing and Claude's "merge partition" verification logic, using conductor-minted proposal IDs.
3. **Phase 3: Pre-Round-1 Pipeline Hooks & `rubric.json` Artifact**: Integrate the rubric proposal/merge loop into `_run_after_activate` (inside the `try` guard of `cli.py`), writing a standalone `rubric.json` post-merge.
4. **Phase 4: Scoring Rounds & Parser Upgrades**: Extend `rounds.py` and `parse_verdict` to parse per-criterion scores (`SCORE cN: [1-5]`) and `RUBRIC-NOTE cN:`.
5. **Phase 5: Gate Integration & Renderers**: Update `board_verdict.py`'s validator (verdict-to-scorecard pinning) and `render_handoff.py` (enforcing D5 byte-identity for non-rubric runs).

---

### 5. Invariants and Guardrails
- **D5 Body Byte-Identity**: The rendered body of `final-consensus.html`/`.md` must remain byte-identical down to zero bytes when `--rubric` is omitted (`scripts/render_handoff.py:45-50`).
- **Sum-to-100 Invariant**: The scorecard validator must strictly verify that the criteria weights are integers that sum to exactly 100.
- **Merge Partition Integrity**: The conductor must verify that the set of proposal IDs minted is partition-identical to the set of IDs listed in the chair's `subsumes` and `drops` outputs.
- **Seat Retries on Invalid Scores**: If a seat fails to output a valid score line (or repeats a score, or outputs an out-of-range integer) for any active criterion, the conductor must trigger the standard two-attempt retry before marking the cell as absent (`—`).

---

### 6. Risks, Stale Assumptions, and Missing Evidence
- **Stale Assumption on `changes`**: The design brief claimed `changes` is reserved for the revision artifact "and not yet defined in the gate validator". However, our reading of `board_verdict.py:180-225` shows that `changes` is *already fully implemented and strictly validated* inside `board_verdict.py`. We must mirror this exact `{artifact, sha256}` pointing style for `scorecard`.
- **Latency of Small Boards**: On a fast-tier, 3-seat board, a developer expects feedback in seconds. Adding proposal and chair-merge calls triples the turnaround time. A static rubric override is vital.
- **Scale Resolution Risk**: Standardizing 1-5 scale allows simple bounding, but we must protect against model drift where seats default to "3" (neutrality bias). We must explicitly define a 5-point rubric anchor prompt for the seats.

---

### 7. Concrete Evidence
- **`board_verdict.py:180-205`**: Shows that the `{artifact, sha256}` pointer style is already robustly established for `changes`.
- **`revision.py:266-285`**: Defines how `choose_revision_seat` performs unique seat-ID matching first, matching on `by_id = {s.id: s for s in config.board}` rather than collapsible provider names.
- **`cli.py:421-435`**: Confirms that `_run_after_activate` starts immediately after setup within the abort guard. The proposal and chair-merge loop must be hooked inside this function to ensure abort/interruption cleanup works.
- **`render_handoff.py:45-50`**: Enforces the strict "zero body bytes when its fields are empty" template-evolution invariant, which must be upheld for the scorecard html sections.

BASIS: evidence
VERDICT: caution
<<<<<<<< END BOARD FINAL-ROUND REVIEWS >>>>>>>>

Produce ONE JSON object — and ONLY that JSON, inside a single ```json``` code fence,
with no prose before or after the fence. Output ONLY the content fields below; do
NOT include any of these conductor-owned keys — board, date, rounds, schema, title — the conductor
will fill those from authoritative state and MERGE your fields in. Any of those
keys you emit will be dropped.

Required content fields:
- `verdict` (string): one of `ship` | `caution` | `block`. Choose the verdict the
  board's final-round tokens collectively support. If all seats agree, use that
  token. If they disagree, weight toward the more cautious token (block > caution
  > ship) — a torn board is the regime where the gate's `abstain` exists, so a
  defensible `block` or `caution` is better than a guessed `ship`.
- `confidence` (string): one of `low` | `medium` | `high`. high = unanimous,
  medium = a clear majority, low = torn or a single voice carrying the call.

Optional content fields (include each only when the reviews support it):
- `decision` (string): the board's call in its OWN domain language, for when "ship"
  reads oddly — e.g. `invest` / `hold` / `wind-down` for a business decision, `accept`
  / `revise` / `reject` for a paper. It becomes the human-facing label verbatim; the
  machine `verdict` stays the gate axis. Set it only when the board's domain has a
  natural word the reviews used; a software-shipping board needs none.
- `blockers` (array of objects): a deduplicated list of the load-bearing objections
  the board raised in the final round. Each object: `title` (short), `body`
  (the seat-grounded reasoning), `evidence` (array — see below).
- `dissent` (array of objects): each `{ "who": seat-name, "body": the seat's
  specific disagreement }`. PRESERVE dissent — never collapse it into consensus.
- `concerns` (array of objects): non-blocking items the board flagged, same shape
  as `blockers`.
- `caveats` (array of strings): "what this review cannot prove" statements the
  board itself made.
- `open_questions` (array of strings): unresolved questions the board raised.
- `next_actions` (array of strings): concrete remediation steps the board
  recommended in the reviews.

Evidence rules (each `evidence[]` item must be one of these typed citations):
- `{ "kind": "code", "path": "...", "line": N }` — a path:line a review cited
  inline. The `line` must be a positive integer.
- `{ "kind": "code", "path": "...", "symbol": "..." }` — a path + a code symbol
  a review cited.
- `{ "kind": "source", "url": "...", "quote": "..." }` — a quote a review pulled
  verbatim from the source (URL or path), with the exact text quoted.
- `{ "kind": "command", "command": "..." }` — a command a review cited as a check.
- `{ "kind": "judgment", "detail": "..." }` — a board judgment with no external
  receipt (an inferred gap, an "absence of X"). Use this when the claim is
  defensible from the reviews but no path:line / quote backs it up — never to
  smuggle in your own opinion.

Strict rules:
- Quote ONLY citations the board's reviews actually contain. Inventing a path,
  symbol, quote, or command is fabrication; if uncertain, use `kind: judgment`
  with a `detail` describing the gap.
- A blocker must rest on at least one piece of evidence (`code` / `source` /
  `command` / `judgment`). A claim with no evidence cannot be a blocker — record
  it as a `concern` instead.
- Do NOT introduce new objections, lenses, or reframing. If you find yourself
  writing a sentence the reviews do not support, delete it.

Reply with the ```json``` fence and nothing else.
